         "id": "v2-69002-p11-practice-slot-6-211127-slot-6-q-a",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "a",
         "prompt": "Using $\\cos(\\sin^{-1}x)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$, show that $\\tan(\\sin^{-1}x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ for $-1<x<1$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 40,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$\\tan(\\sin^{-1}x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, for $-1<x<1$.",
          "worked": "Let $\\alpha=\\sin^{-1}x$, so $\\sin\\alpha=x$ and $\\cos\\alpha=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$, which is non-zero for $-1<x<1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\cos\\alpha}\\\\&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}.\\end{align*}$$"
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "v2-69002-p11-practice-slot-6-211127-slot-6-q-b",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "b",
         "prompt": "Hence sketch the graph of $y=\\tan(\\sin^{-1}x)$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 80,
         "answer": {
          "short": "The odd, increasing curve $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ through the origin on $-1<x<1$, with vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=1$; range $\\mathbb R$.",
          "worked": "From part a, $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ with $-1<x<1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y(0)&=0\\\\y(-x)&=\\frac{-x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}=-y(x)\\end{align*}$$\nThe graph is odd and passes through $(0,0)$.\n\nAs $x\\to1^-$, the numerator tends to 1 and the denominator tends to $0^+$, so $y\\to\\infty$. By symmetry, $y\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-1^+$.\n\nDraw the increasing curve between the vertical asymptotes $x=\\pm1$.",
