  "be2b7fb5-d1a5-4e3c-9664-5bd743e0839c": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p5-definition-text",
     "replacement": "- Sine, cosine and tangent **fail the horizontal line test**, so their inverse relations are not functions.\n- To create an **inverse trigonometric function**, **restrict the domain** to an interval where the curve is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing and still takes every value in the function's range.\n- Mathematicians chose the simplest such intervals: $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ for sine, $[0,\\pi]$ for cosine and $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ for tangent.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p5-definition-text",
     "targetFingerprint": "f42f2f25314de248a1a41e35daf50ec0f437ee35d630c971ac566d1963417cb0",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-inverse-trigonometric-functions-v1/20261110-211127",
   "revision": 14
  },
  "c0f67cef-564d-4e42-b65f-898b546b9d23": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p7-method",
     "replacement": "1. Find the **reference angle**: the angle in $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ whose ratio has the same size as the input. If the input is outside the domain of the inverse function, the value is undefined.\n2. Find the angle in the range of the inverse function that has the input as its ratio.\n\nWe always work in radians.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p7-method",
     "targetFingerprint": "25abda2d01a5f50502f4266d38743ff566edf982e919bc35082431bbf4a7ad30",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-inverse-trigonometric-functions-v1/20261110-211127",
   "revision": 14
  },
  "4c6ec968-a2b6-486f-aec8-a7992f89c852": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p10-identities-block",
     "replacement": "For inverse sine and cosine, $-1\\le x\\le1$; the inverse-tangent identity holds for all real $x$. In each reason, $\\theta$ is the inverse function's output for $x$.\n\n$\\sin^{-1}(-x)=-\\sin^{-1}x$\tbecause $\\sin(-\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta$.\n$\\cos^{-1}(-x)=\\pi-\\cos^{-1}x$\tbecause $\\cos(\\pi-\\theta)=-\\cos\\theta$.\n$\\tan^{-1}(-x)=-\\tan^{-1}x$\tbecause $\\tan(-\\theta)=-\\tan\\theta$.\n$\\sin^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\tbecause $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta\\right)=\\sin\\theta$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p10-identities-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "bdc4a2d55195b3286bdc12af070fbfff0c2d01c0da4b9ce93d84d3dada9ba7a2",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-inverse-trigonometric-functions-v1/20261110-211127",
   "revision": 14
  },
  "5a06ec90-453d-4af8-b45a-014ed8c6cad0": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p16-teaching",
     "replacement": "$y=\\sin^{-1}(x-d)+c$, and likewise for $\\cos^{-1}$ and $\\tan^{-1}$\n$d$: horizontal translation $d$ units right (left if $d<0$)\n$c$: vertical translation $c$ units up (down if $c<0$)",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p16-teaching",
     "targetFingerprint": "3876748554b38106453fa45d58fa0b14c5bc418c40b2680f94c3c4cdb9a4a293",
     "applied": true
    }
