  "a9709348-b9ce-421a-b622-6e4678c78917": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p22-teaching-compositions",
     "replacement": "A trigonometric function undoes its inverse wherever the inverse is defined.\n\n$\\sin(\\sin^{-1}x)=x$\tfor $-1\\le x\\le1$\n$\\cos(\\cos^{-1}x)=x$\tfor $-1\\le x\\le1$\n$\\tan(\\tan^{-1}x)=x$\tfor $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$\n\nFor $x$ outside $[-1,1]$, $\\sin(\\sin^{-1}x)$ and $\\cos(\\cos^{-1}x)$ are undefined.\n\nNote: $\\tan^{-1}x$ accepts every real $x$, so $\\tan(\\tan^{-1}x)$ is never undefined.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p22-teaching-compositions",
     "targetFingerprint": "82acb2401b5942e1e17afaf0f9104799927e7921789875eff94943b7639337a9",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-inverse-trigonometric-functions-v1/20261110-211127",
   "revision": 14
  },
  "e2e34be6-b8ce-4a87-9e3b-3c121b1627d6": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p26-teaching",
     "replacement": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\sin^{-1}(\\sin x)&=x&\\text{for }-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le x\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\[4pt]\\cos^{-1}(\\cos x)&=x&\\text{for }0\\le x\\le\\pi\\\\[4pt]\\tan^{-1}(\\tan x)&=x&\\text{for }-\\frac{\\pi}{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf $x$ is outside the given interval and the inner trigonometric function is defined, find the equivalent angle within the given range:\n\n1. Determine the reference angle.\n2. Find the equivalent angle: the angle in the range that has the same value of the inner function. Check the sign.\n  $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{For }\\sin^{-1}(\\sin x),&\\quad -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le x\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\quad\\text{(Q1, Q4)}\\\\[4pt]\\text{For }\\cos^{-1}(\\cos x),&\\quad 0\\le x\\le\\pi\\quad\\text{(Q1, Q2)}\\\\[4pt]\\text{For }\\tan^{-1}(\\tan x),&\\quad -\\frac{\\pi}{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\quad\\text{(Q1, Q4)}\\end{aligned}$$\n3. Rewrite the inner trig function with the equivalent angle.\n4. Evaluate using the identities above.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p26-teaching",
